WEBVTT

00:00:01.053 --> 00:00:04.286
A reader brings two identical books to the desk.

00:00:04.627 --> 00:00:07.678
You scan the first, lend it out, and the second

00:00:07.678 --> 00:00:10.600
disappears from the list of available copies.

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:14.212
The app did exactly what the code told it to do.

00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:17.823
Scan the code on screen to try this case yourself.

00:00:19.821 --> 00:00:23.342
An AI assistant can make that mistake in a prototype.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:28.082
An experienced engineer can make it in a carefully tested system.

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:34.221
If the test data contains only one copy per edition, every test can pass.

00:00:34.526 --> 00:00:37.291
An ISBN identifies the edition.

00:00:37.596 --> 00:00:40.575
When I lend a book, I lend one physical copy.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.893
That distinction changes who gets to borrow the book.

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.127
With OnToGraPh’s agent-ready ontology, an

00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:50.891
agent can check what a code means before acting.

00:00:51.135 --> 00:00:55.493
Charge this expense to WBS 0017.

00:00:55.737 --> 00:00:58.525
In Finance, that code identifies a posting object.

00:00:58.768 --> 00:01:01.385
In the project office, it identifies a work package.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:03.970
The agent pauses and asks: Which department

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.311
do you mean, Finance or the project office?

00:01:06.555 --> 00:01:10.011
The agent waits for your answer. The expense stays on hold.

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:16.286
On the homepage, open Hidden Risks and choose Describe my app.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.042
Here is a different brief.

00:01:18.376 --> 00:01:21.801
A community group assigns volunteers to shelters.

00:01:22.135 --> 00:01:24.459
A night shift is a type of shift.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.994
A shift depends on a volunteer.

00:01:27.328 --> 00:01:29.480
Generate the proposal.

00:01:29.814 --> 00:01:33.392
The browser uses local language patterns and keeps the

00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:36.837
description in this tab until you open it in Studio.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.610
Look at the suggested risk.

00:01:40.928 --> 00:01:45.848
If the same assignment arrives twice, is it a retry or a second shift?

00:01:46.166 --> 00:01:49.038
The dictionary proposes terms from the brief.

00:01:49.355 --> 00:01:51.286
The taxonomy groups them.

00:01:51.604 --> 00:01:55.594
The ontology connects the assignment, volunteer and shelter.

00:01:55.912 --> 00:01:57.371
These are suggestions.

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:00.011
The person who schedules the shifts can

00:02:00.011 --> 00:02:02.868
correct a definition before anyone builds on it.

00:02:04.855 --> 00:02:07.294
Choose Review in Studio and sign in.

00:02:07.637 --> 00:02:10.291
The case opens in its own workspace.

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:13.264
Expand a dictionary entry, edit the

00:02:13.264 --> 00:02:16.871
definition, apply the change and save the draft.

00:02:17.214 --> 00:02:20.567
Reopen the workspace to see the saved revision.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.434
You can also download the model as JSON and use the

00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:27.820
standalone Studio locally with Python and SQLite.

00:02:29.853 --> 00:02:34.834
Now consider two departments using the same project code.

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:39.017
In our recorded pilot, a spreadsheet acknowledged a new

00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:43.278
rule and still counted two thousand euros of eligible budget.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.083
The corrected workbook counted eight hundred.

00:02:47.424 --> 00:02:51.166
After withdrawal it still counted eight hundred.

00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:55.957
A later reading of zero verified withdrawal for those records.

00:02:56.299 --> 00:02:58.245
The applications were real.

00:02:58.587 --> 00:03:01.823
The records and review roles were synthetic.

00:03:03.825 --> 00:03:06.765
The guided journeys cover a personal library,

00:03:06.765 --> 00:03:10.881
departmental codes, a glossary for Copilot, and policy changes.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.698
Each has a starting model and checks.

00:03:14.008 --> 00:03:17.027
In Weighted Relationships, compare confidence in the

00:03:17.027 --> 00:03:19.872
evidence with the consequence of a wrong mapping.

00:03:20.182 --> 00:03:24.042
Use the architecture puzzle to explore missing components.

00:03:24.351 --> 00:03:28.382
In a delivery pipeline, the policy compiler turns a reviewed

00:03:28.382 --> 00:03:32.884
rule into an artifact that can be tested in continuous integration.

00:03:34.826 --> 00:03:37.495
Different tools answer different questions.

00:03:37.805 --> 00:03:41.635
NVIDIA Auto Ontology builds semantic reasoning layers.

00:03:41.946 --> 00:03:45.891
OntoBricks maps ontologies onto Databricks tables.

00:03:46.202 --> 00:03:49.549
Genie answers business questions using governed data.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.804
Stardog supports graph queries and inference.

00:03:53.115 --> 00:03:55.530
Drools executes business rules.

00:03:55.841 --> 00:04:00.265
OntoGraph connects a reviewed meaning to the behavior observed in the

00:04:00.265 --> 00:04:04.882
receiving application, including what happens after a rule is withdrawn.

00:04:06.766 --> 00:04:10.666
OnToGraPh began as the shared ontology layer for PALO Framework and

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:14.565
PolicyWatcher. Today this open-source framework runs independently.

00:04:14.809 --> 00:04:18.667
If an external policy changes, PolicyWatcher can supply source evidence.

00:04:18.911 --> 00:04:22.364
If a decision needs a governance review, PALO can receive the case.

00:04:22.607 --> 00:04:25.726
In the recorded integration pilot, PolicyWatcher was read

00:04:25.726 --> 00:04:29.117
only and a local PALO case remained open for a human decision.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.011
Opening an optional path sends nothing from your draft.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.998
Pick one term two people use differently.

00:04:37.312 --> 00:04:41.273
Write one example that should pass and another that should be held.

00:04:41.587 --> 00:04:44.138
Scan the first code to try your own app.

00:04:44.453 --> 00:04:49.229
The second takes you to the source, sample models and local setup.

00:04:49.543 --> 00:04:53.364
Bring the result back to the person who owns the decision.

00:04:53.678 --> 00:04:55.635
That is where a working prototype

00:04:55.635 --> 00:04:58.244
becomes a model you can trust for that case.
